From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: server: a pipe, event, mutex, semaphore, timer or section created without a descriptor has its creator as owner

On Windows these objects, created with no security descriptor, are still
owned by their creator: owner and group come from the creating token. Wine
kept no descriptor, and NtQuerySecurityObject returned an empty one. The
Cygwin/MSYS runtime takes a pty's owner from its pipes' security
(cygpsid::get_id) and dereferenced the NULL owner: every program run in Git
for Windows' terminal (bash, tty, sh) died of a segmentation fault.

Their creators now record the effective token's owner and primary group
when no descriptor is given. Only those: no DACL is added, and an object
without a DACL grants everyone full access exactly as one without a
descriptor did, so no access check changes (this matters on the shared
multi-user server).

Only these kinds. A first version did it in create_named_object for
everything, and registry keys, window stations, desktops, directories and
links have defaults of their own that an owner-only descriptor overrode:
keys became open to everyone, the descriptors were inherited into the saved
registry, and the session's display links broke (the taskbar laid out for
1024x768).

Gate: test/objowner-gate.sh -- each of these kinds is owned by its creator
with no DACL; a registry key keeps its DACL; a process without
Administrators still opens them fully.
